#: of pyvista.core.filters.poly_data.PolyDataFilters.triangulate_contours:6
msgid ""
"This filter will generate triangles to fill all of the 2D contours in its "
"input. The input to the filter is a set of lines (not polylines) which when "
"joined form loops. The contours may be concave, and may even contain holes "
"i.e. a contour may contain an internal contour that is wound in the opposite"
" direction (as compared to the outer polygon normal) to indicate that it is "
"a hole."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/filters/poly_data.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.filters.poly_data.PolyDataFilters.triangulate_contours:15
msgid ""
"This filter will assume that the input polygons lie in the same plane and "
"will not perform any projection or transformation of the input data. You may"
" need to project your data to a plane before using this filter."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/filters/poly_data.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.filters.poly_data.PolyDataFilters.triangulate_contours:22
msgid ""
"The triangulation of is done in O(n) time for simple convex inputs, but for "
"non-convex inputs the worst-case time is O(n^2*m^2) where n is the number of"
" points and m is the number of holes. The best triangulation algorithms, in "
"contrast, are O(n log n). The resulting triangles may be quite narrow, the "
"algorithm does not attempt to produce high-quality triangles."
msgstr ""

