#: of pyvista.core.celltype.CellType:4
msgid ""
"Cells are defined by specifying a type in combination with an ordered list "
"of points. The ordered list, often referred to as the connectivity list, "
"combined with the type specification, implicitly defines the topology of the"
" cell. The x-y-z point coordinates define the cell geometry."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/celltype.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.celltype.CellType:9
msgid ""
"Although point coordinates are defined in three dimensions, the cell "
"topology can be 0, 1, 2, or 3-dimensional. Use :attr:`dimension` to check "
"the topological dimension."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/celltype.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.celltype.CellType:12
msgid ""
"Cells can be primary (e.g. triangle) or composite (e.g. triangle strip). "
"Composite cells consist of one or more primary cells, while primary cells "
"cannot be decomposed. Use :attr:`is_composite` to check if a cell type is "
"primary or composite."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/celltype.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.celltype.CellType:16
msgid ""
"Cells can also be characterized as linear or non-linear. Linear cells use "
"linear or constant interpolation, while non-linear cells may use quadratic, "
"cubic, or some other interpolation. Use :attr:`is_linear` to check if a cell"
" type is linear."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../../../../../opt/hostedtoolcache/Python/3.13.14/x64/lib/python3.13/site-packages/pyvista/core/celltype.pydocstring
#: of pyvista.core.celltype.CellType:20
msgid ""
"Higher order interpolation is possible using Lagrange or Bézier cells. For "
"Lagrange cells, all the points lie on the interpolation curve, whereas for "
"the Bézier cells, only the points at the extremities are interpolatory."
msgstr ""

