#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:4
msgid ""
"Python allows arbitrarily setting new attributes on objects at any time, but"
" PyVista's classes do not always allow this. By default, setting a new "
"attribute is only allowed when the attribute's name has a leading underscore"
" \"``_``\", i.e. it is a private attribute; attempting to set a new public "
"attribute raises an ``AttributeError``."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:10
msgid ""
"This context manager may be used to allow or disallow setting `any` new "
"attribute, public or private, either globally or within a context."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:17
msgid "**mode** : 'private' | :ref:`bool <python:bltin-boolean-values>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes-1
msgid "'private' |"
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:18
msgid "Control if setting new attributes is allowed."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:20
msgid ""
"``'private'``: Allow setting private attributes, but do not allow setting "
"public attributes."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:22
msgid "``True``: Allow setting any new attribute, either private or public."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:23
msgid ""
"``False``: Do not allow setting new attributes, regardless if they are "
"private or public."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:25
msgid ""
"``'private'`` is used by default, ``True`` removes all restrictions, and "
"``False`` is most strict."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:30
msgid ""
"An attribute is considered private if its name has a leading underscore "
"\"``_``\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:45
msgid ":obj:`pyvista.set_new_attribute`"
msgstr ":obj:`pyvista.set_new_attribute`"

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:46
msgid "Function for setting any new attribute on a PyVista object."
msgstr ""

#: ../docstring of pyvista.allow_new_attributes:52
msgid ""
"Allowing new attributes by default outside ``__init__`` and/or without "
"formally defining class properties was found to be a source of bugs for both"
" developers and users of PyVista. It's very easy to set the wrong attribute "
"name (e.g. ``interpolate`` vs. ``interpolation``) without any errors being "
"generated, and users transitioning from older versions of PyVista would have"
" code that sets attributes which were once valid, but have since been "
"deprecated and removed. Attempting to set an attribute which is not already "
"defined now raises an ``AttributeError`` to clearly signal that there is a "
"potential problem with this line of code."
msgstr ""

